###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Force-evaluation speedups of the cheap over the expensive potentials: about 30x (Si ACE), 75x (W UF3), 8x (Fe ACE), 4.4x (W ACE 3,14 vs 3,21, about 5,000 atoms, CPU) and 29x (W SNAP vs W-He MACE, GPU); mixed-simulation speedups in Tables 4-5. Constrained fitting takes about 5 minutes on one CPU core.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Table 2 energy errors are per atom (column header 'meV'). The W SNAP fitting data are not public, so it has no RMSE. The W UF3 RMSEs on the full DFT data refer to a fit with identical hyperparameters directly to DFT, not the constrained model used in the mixed simulations.
# - Speedups are ratios, not absolute timings. Elastic constants and lattice parameters (Table 3), NEB barriers, diffusion coefficients and dislocation velocities are properties.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/birks2026.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

